90l|a|Having the color of the clear sky, or a hue resembling it, whether lighter or darker; as, the deep, blue sea; as blue as a sapphire; blue violets. ; Pale, without redness or glare, -- said of a flame; hence, of the color of burning brimstone, betokening the presence of ghosts or devils; as, the candle burns blue; the air was blue with oaths. ; Low in spirits; melancholy; as, to feel blue. ; Suited to produce low spirits; gloomy in prospect; as, thongs looked blue. ; Severe or over strict in morals; gloom; as, blue and sour religionists; suiting one who is over strict in morals; inculcating an impracticable, severe, or gloomy mortality; as, blue laws. ; Literary; -- applied to women; -- an abbreviation of bluestocking.	j:1ko4,121,67;21fa,106,4;27l0,100,10;27kt,96,127;1ko2,92,3;29r3,91,12;ukw,89,2;21im,86,22;298i,84,4;zgx,82,21;1l2f,78,4;52y,78,20;eyv,77,2;edj,75,7;nwb,73,22~27kt,96,127;19wv,68,69;1ko4,121,67;1yxc,41,52;19d0,68,46;1a21,40,33;1s7,49,32;6u8,56,31;dvh,72,29;aqi,42,29;21im,86,22;nwb,73,22;zgx,82,21;52y,78,20;1a2o,41,17
